 them about the girl who was murdered. West also offered them services to help them. The services could help them avoid getting involved in serious crimes again. The people who needed help most were the ones who were on the list. "It's not a threat. We're giving you information. Not just the law enforcement information.... information that there's no excuses for anyone to be out there shooting and killing," Mallette said. For instance, Jacobi Herring, who was the 21-year-old student was killed walking home last month, made the heat list. The police believed that he belonged on the list. They were right. Herring was shot by a man who is not yet known. He has been in contact with other people who police suspected of a crime. Herring had been arrested only a small number of times. Four other people, however, had also made the list. Herring's death made it clear that he needed help from police. Cook County court records showed that he had been arrested a few times. However, the four other people who were on the list had been arrested even more. One of them was shot and killed only weeks before Herring was killed. ## No One Knows Why People Choose A Life Of Crime The message police were giving to these people could have a hard time winning over some young men like Herring. Young men who grew up in the inner city and who grow up in violence are fatalistic, Lurigio said. This means they will never believe that anything bad is going to happen to them. Young men like these are generally not easily discouraged. Police officers, therefore, did not believe that West's warnings would affect people like Herring. To kick off the program last month in her district, West knocked on the doors of people on the heat list in Austin over about a two-week period. She talked about their criminal records and the bad people they know. She offered them services to help them change. They could receive therapy. People on the list could also learn how to make money. "Some of them... acknowledged